# Architecture

```
fleet-live/
├── apps/api/          Express 5 + SQLite (node:sqlite)
├── apps/web/          React 19 + Vite + React Router
└── packages/shared/   Domain types, Zod validation, list/SSE contracts
```

Data flow: React `--HTTP + SSE /api-->` Express (`app.ts` + `routes/`) → controllers → models → SQLite.

`@fleet-live/shared` is the contract. Do not duplicate vehicle, list-query, telemetry, or stream schemas in api or web.

## Boundaries

- UI never accesses SQLite or API models.
- Models never take `req` / `res`.
- `apps/web/src/components/ui` is generic. Vehicle logic lives in `components/vehicles`, hooks, and `api/`.
- Live updates are SSE (`GET /api/stream`, `POST /api/stream/focus`), not WebSockets and not list polling.

## Current vs planned

**Implemented:** vehicle CRUD; server-driven list (search/filter/sort/pagination/facets); SSE telemetry with per-connection focus; telemetry history API; trips with the driven path as an encoded polyline (`GET /api/vehicles/:id/trips/latest`); vehicle list/detail/CRUD UI; Leaflet detail map (marker, SSE, trail from the trip); fleet map (`/fleet`, last positions in the viewport, live ticks for driving vehicles in view); route simulation with a city/highway speed profile and fuel consumption; simulation pause via `GET`/`PATCH /api/sim`; companies; session login (`POST /api/auth/login`); tenant isolation by `company_id`; roles (`dispatcher` / `viewer`); per-company retention for closed trips (`TRIP_RETENTION_DAYS`).

**Not implemented — do not treat as present:** alerts REST/UI, frontend tests, CI/CD.

The fleet map is `/fleet`, not the list page. After login it loads last positions for the session company in the visible bbox (`GET /api/vehicles/positions`). The driver picker is an optional view filter, not a requirement. Status chips and plate search apply to that company snapshot. If more than `FLEET_POSITIONS_MAX` match, `truncated` is set and no markers are drawn — not a sample of the first 2000 ids. `drivers` is a view filter, not a saved group. The list stays a query page. Unauthenticated vehicle, stream, and sim requests are `401`. Other companies’ vehicles are `404`, not listed. License plates are unique per company. SSE `vehicles-changed` and telemetry patches are delivered only to connections of the same company; `POST /api/stream/focus` cannot take over another company’s connection. `GET`/`PATCH /api/sim` pauses simulation for the session company only. Trips have no `company_id`; access is `trip → vehicle → company` (`TripModel.latestForVehicle` joins `vehicles`). `company_id` on create/update is taken from the session, never from the request body. `dispatcher` may create/update/delete vehicles and `PATCH /api/sim`. `viewer` gets `403` on those writes; reads (list, detail, map, stream, `GET /api/sim`) stay allowed within the company.

## Two layers of position data

- `telemetry` is the **hot buffer**: last known position for list and marker, plus the newest movement. Bounded by `TELEMETRY_KEEP_PER_VEHICLE` (100). Rows are meant to expire.
- `trips` is the **durable record**: one row per drive, the path as an encoded polyline (`@fleet-live/shared` `encodePoint` / `decodePolyline`). A 300 km haul is one row of a few KB. Closed trips older than `TRIP_RETENTION_DAYS` (default 90) are deleted per company (`TripModel.pruneClosedForCompany` joins `vehicles`; no `company_id` on `trips`). Open trips are kept. Prune runs after trip close and after a telemetry tick for companies in that batch.
- Never size the visible trail by a point count. That was the old bug: how far you could see depended on the tick rate, not on the drive.
- Appending stays O(1): `path = path || ?` in SQL, predecessor in `last_latitude` / `last_longitude`. The simulator appends the **road vertices** between ticks (`verticesBetween`), not the chord. Do not read the path back into Node per tick.
- `TripModel.close` simplifies with Ramer-Douglas-Peucker (`lib/geo.ts`, 12 m). `distance_m` keeps the sum of reported segments, not the length of the simplified line.
- Live map: SSE `path_delta` is an encoded suffix; the marker slides along the new vertices. Telemetry remains the hot position only. `path_reset` starts a new line (new trip) instead of concatenating.
- The simulator closes the open trip when it reaches a route end and turns around. The return is a new trip, not a second layer on the same path.

## Domain rules

- `status` is what the vehicle reports. The UI shows it read-only. Do not offer start/stop trip in the frontend — a real fleet does not decide that, and the simulator already drives `DRIVING` vehicles. Pause **this company’s** simulation with `PATCH /api/sim` (not the whole process).
- `DRIVING` opens a trip, any other status closes it (`syncTrip` in the vehicle controller). `TripModel.appendPoints` also opens one lazily, so seeded or imported driving vehicles still get a path.
- Ending a trip writes a final telemetry point with speed `0` (`TelemetryModel.recordStandstill`).
- `fuel_level` is measured while `DRIVING` (simulator) and manually maintainable otherwise. Do not overwrite a measured value with a stale form value.
- A trip has no per-point speed or time by design. Do not add parallel arrays; aggregates (`distance_m`, `max_speed`) live on the trip.

The `users` table backs login. `role` is `dispatcher` or `viewer`. Isolation is company, not driver. The `alerts` table and `active_alerts` count/filter exist; there is no `GET /api/alerts`.

## Source of truth

- Code is what exists. If README and code disagree, code wins. README Current Status / Roadmap should describe the running system, not a future plan.
- `apps/docs/table.md` describes the table component, not a feature backlog.

## New work

- Reuse existing patterns before new libraries or layers.
- User-facing copy (UI and API `error`/`fields`) is German; machine `code` values stay English.
